be, you gotta pick up

00:11:56.721 --> 00:11:58.670
this kid from here and you
gotta take this kid here.

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:00.400
Yeah, it's bull crap.

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:04.430
And we didn't do the
hop, skip and Uber thing

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:06.060
that you did, so we never got into that

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:09.383
because it was wasn't for
our family, okay, whatever.

00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:12.090
So, we were takin' 'em around.

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:14.423
It's like, I gotta stay on it.

00:12:14.423 --> 00:12:17.280
But, she wasn't motivated.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:21.250
She's got a friend who I heard
her friend say it herself.

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:23.230
She's like, at this
point, I'm just waiting

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:26.011
until I turn 18 to get my
license because at that point

00:12:26.011 --> 00:12:28.530
you can just get a license and there's

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:29.580
no restrictions on it.

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:31.520
You don't have to go
through all the rigamarole.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:32.863
I just don't understand that.

00:12:32.863 --> 00:12:35.358
Freedom, automobile represents freedom.

00:12:35.358 --> 00:12:40.358
I know, and I had a really hard time,

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:47.060
I was not successful at giving Lilly,

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:50.830
to transfer any excitement,
any vision for that freedom

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:53.830
that we experienced when we turned 16.

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:55.383
We're just chompin' at the bit.

00:12:55.383 --> 00:12:59.023
I mean, in October, when you turned 16.

00:12:59.023 --> 00:13:01.070
I was at the DMV on my birthday.

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:03.490
Oh my gosh, man, and
I was waitin' for you,

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:04.717
you came to my house and picked me up

00:13:04.717 --> 00:13:06.383
and we just rode nowhere.

00:13:06.383 --> 00:13:08.015
It was wonderful.

00:13:08.015 --> 00:13:12.470
The only thing that I
thought about for months.

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:14.490
Yeah, and we just drove around.

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:16.610
The idea that it's not a priority, again,

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.920
I know it's a different time.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:21.220
I've heard this from many parents,

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:22.760
they're having trouble with their kids,

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:24.490
getting their kids to
care about, even kids

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:25.530
back in North Carolina.

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:28.499
Yeah, it's not just an
LA thing and you easily

00:13:28.499 --> 00:13:31.523
just reduce it to like,
well, it's the screens.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:35.960
And I think there is a
level of connectivity

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:39.150
that defrays the need to get in the car

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.480
and meet up with people or show up places

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:44.500
because you're constantly
into each other's feeds,

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:46.340
so you're constantly in each other's faces

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:50.423
or you're constantly in each
other's video game thingies.

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:52.980
Video game thingies.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:55.350
But, even once we got in
the car and we started

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:57.550
driving around and realized
we had nowhere to go,

00:13:57.550 --> 00:13:58.840
we weren't complaining.

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:01.690
We were reveling in just
driving nowhere for hours

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:06.690
listening to tapes and I
never really transferred

00:14:07.460 --> 00:14:08.900
that passion to Lilly.

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:09.760
I just started to institute.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:10.593
What do you mean trans, you can't.

00:14:10.593 --> 00:14:11.880
That's not how it works.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:15.721
No, I just started
instituting levels of bribery

00:14:15.721 --> 00:14:18.968
and control.

00:14:18.968 --> 00:14:22.253
Yeah, there's many
ultimatums that I've made.

00:14:22.253 --> 00:14:27.253
Once you wouldn't be able
to drive yourself somewhere,

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:31.477
at that point you're
responsible for getting there

00:14:31.477 --> 00:14:33.340
and that means financially, too.

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:35.400
You know, that's what I've said.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:36.290
Okay.

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:39.510
I think this is where you
shot yourself in the foot

00:14:40.850 --> 00:14:45.170
because among many other
things, like I said, bribery,

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:50.000
if you make it, you gotta do
like a 40 or 50 hour course

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:52.350
and then pass the test before you can get

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:53.673
your learner's permit.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:56.446
Then you gotta go to the DMV.

00:14:56.446 --> 00:14:58.670
It's never simple

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:00.070
at any point in the process.

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:04.730
But, because we never did
the Uber or there was never

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:06.860
any way for our kids
to get anywhere unless

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:11.860
we took 'em, and so I started to say,

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:14.170
when Lilly would say, I need to go here

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:17.090
or I need to pick up a
friend or I have to show up

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:19.340
for this thing or can you pick me up here,

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:24.340
I would be like, no, but I will point out,

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:31.510
if you had your license you could do this.

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:33.950
So, she started to feel the pain

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:36.390
of not being able to do some things

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:37.223
that she wanted to do.

00:15:37.223 --> 00:15:39.410
It didn't happen a lot,
but it did happen some.

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:41.750
So, you don't have that card to play

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:43.540
because of the Uber thing.

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:45.010
He's not gonna pay, he
doesn't have any money

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:45.843
to pay for it.

00:15:45.843 --> 00:15:49.050
Okay, so you just, then that does work.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:51.390
He might be able to go what, one place.

00:15:51.390 --> 00:15:53.000
Have you been doin' that?

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.610
No, I said when it,
I'm gonna, I don't know

00:15:56.610 --> 00:15:57.443
what the timeline is.

00:15:57.443 --> 00:15:58.410
Right now.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.520
For when it's like, okay now at this point

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:02.660
I'm not taking you to this place

00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:04.380
and if you wanna go
you have to pay for it.

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:05.213
Now.

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:09.010
Okay, I have to get my
wife onboard with this.

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:09.900
Here's the thing.

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.080
I don't know how your
wife thought about this.

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:15.920
My wife is so concerned about their safety

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:18.650
that she's like, you know, I don't know

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:19.483
if I want him driving in LA.

00:16:19.483 --> 00:16:24.350
Like, what, he's gonna drive
in Los Angeles at some point.

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:27.825
Yeah, but the thing that
Christy told me was,

00:16:27.825 --> 00:16:31.130
she was like, well, when
she does get her license

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:32.980
she can't drive that Sion, she's gotta get

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:34.350
a car with a backup camera.

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:36.917
And I'm like, backup camera?

00:16:36.917 --> 00:16:41.190
I backed up, if you look at
all the times I've backed up

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:44.960
over the years, you could
back up around the moon twice

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:46.720
in the amount of times I've
backed up and I never once

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:47.553
had a camera.

00:16:47.553 --> 00:16:49.848
It's questionable, but
you backed up a lot.

00:16:49.848 --> 00:16:50.681
Never once had a camera.

00:16:50.681 --> 00:16:52.470
In fact, you should learn to back up

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:54.590
without the camera before
you start using the camera.

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:55.740
'Cause I can tell you right now.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:56.760
Cameras sometimes it doesn't work.

00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:58.410
Sometimes it's raining and it gets blurry,

00:16:58.410 --> 00:16:59.243
it gets foggy.

00:16:59.243 --> 00:17:00.950
You gotta know how to back
up a car without the camera.

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:05.760
In fact, if you give your kid a car

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:06.930
that's got a backup camera.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:08.040
You're handicappin' 'em.

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:09.683
At the beginning you need to put tape

00:17:09.683 --> 00:17:11.549
over that backup camera.

00:17:11.549 --> 00:17:14.720
Put tape over the backup
camera until they prove

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:16.702
that they can back up
and then take it off.

00:17:16.702 --> 00:17:17.535
Like Stevie's webcam?

00:17:17.535 --> 00:17:18.518
Stevie puts a Post-It note over a webcam.

00:17:18.518 --> 00:17:19.680
Right.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:23.080
Now, I am exclusively reliant
upon the backup camera.

00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:26.244
I mean, I can back up
like at 78 miles per hour.

00:17:26.244 --> 00:17:27.077
Like a grandma.

00:17:27.077 --> 00:17:27.910
You can back 70?

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:28.844
I come down my driveway,
you know my driveway's

00:17:28.844 --> 00:17:30.810
kinda steep and nuts.

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:32.380
Yeah, you don't give it a bit of gas

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:36.430
and you're going, you're
accelerating like a rocket.

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:38.490
Put the pedal to the metal
going downhill backwards.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:40.075
Man, if there was a trashcan down there

00:17:40.075 --> 00:17:41.650
you would obliterate it.

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:45.493
So, why was today the
first day that she drove?

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:48.140
She's had her license.

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:49.090
She hasn't wanted to?

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:50.780
She's had her license for a few months

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:53.190
and she's been well, to park at school,

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:56.340
it's all curb parking
and they do not teach

00:17:56.340 --> 00:18:00.300
curb parking, they do not
require parallel parking

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:02.714
or curb parking as part
of the driving test.

00:18:02.714 --> 00:18:04.075
Yeah.

00:18:04.075 --> 00:18:08.090
So, I had to take her out
and start teaching her

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:10.510
to park on the curb to
prepare her, give her

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:12.860
the confidence to park and go to school.

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:14.520
There's no parking lot.

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:15.353
It's just street parking.

00:18:15.353 --> 00:18:20.310
So, I took her out and first of all,

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:21.940
I never tell her, I'm just like,

00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:22.850
we're going for a drive.

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:24.360
And then, I'm like okay,
I want you to park here.

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:26.550
It's like, dad, I don't
know how to parallel park.

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:28.080
It's like, exactly.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:29.250
Now we're gonna do it.

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:30.412
Kids man.

00:18:30.412 --> 00:18:33.280
It's like, why do they
say things like that?

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:35.064
Dad, I don't know how to parallel park.

00:18:35.064 --> 00:18:37.501
Why the hell do you think we're here?

00:18:37.501 --> 00:18:42.197
And are you okay with just
continuing on in your inability?

00:18:42.197 --> 00:18:43.890
I don't understand.

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:45.930
I don't think that we were like that.

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:46.763
That firm.

00:18:46.763 --> 00:18:48.680
Kids, these days.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:51.640
And that is the reason why I was the one

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:53.169
doin' it and not Christy 'cause Christy,

00:18:53.169 --> 00:18:58.169
she doesn't want to fuel Lilly's anxiety

00:18:59.750 --> 00:19:01.675
as she's learning, but I'm like,

00:19:01.675 --> 00:19:04.910
I'm kinda fed up so I'm very firm.

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:07.090
But, she responded positively

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:09.880
and she parallel parked.

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:12.563
I mean, we did it you know, 10 times.

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:16.500
And then, I was like, okay
now you can drive to school.

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:17.790
She's like, I don't feel comfortable.

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:22.270
So then, before the break
every day that I could

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:25.500
I would ride with her to school

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:27.640
and tell her where to park and I would

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:31.060
just be there to guide her through it.

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:34.940
But, we basically found a way,

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:37.240
a place where she could park on the curb

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:40.690
and be the last car so she wouldn't have

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:42.120
to do the parallel parking thing.

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:45.160
So, we found that and we
did that for a few days.

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:49.551
So yesterday, she was
parking, getting outta the car

00:19:49.551 --> 00:19:51.580
and I was gettin' in and
driving the car back.

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:53.270
There was a couple of weeks of that.

00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:55.623
Did she know that today was the day?

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:58.450
Yesterday she knew that, yeah.

00:19:59.350 --> 00:20:01.001
And she did it and I was,

00:20:01.001 --> 00:20:04.000
I wasn't totally ready.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:05.410
I was in the shower and I was like,

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:07.440
oh man, she's about to leave for school

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:08.570
and I don't want Christy to take her.

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:10.130
I wanna make sure that she goes,

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:12.540
so I put a towel around my waist

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:13.790
and I got outta the shower

00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:17.110
and I run downstairs as
she's yelling at Lincoln,

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:18.420
we gotta go, it's time to go,

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.200
which is every morning.

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:23.320
And they, so then I met
'em in my front yard.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:24.710
I'm wearing nothin' but a towel

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:26.720
and I'm like, you can do this.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:27.980
You can park.

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:30.050
You can go to school on your own.

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:31.522
Let's take a photo.

00:20:31.522 --> 00:20:35.010
She refused the photo.

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:35.843
Smart girl.

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:38.440
But, I'm very proud of Lilly.

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:42.010
She did, I am proud of
myself, but I'm proud

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:44.396
of her, too, because she responded.

00:20:44.396 --> 00:20:46.660
And you know what?

00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:50.340
She's startin' to get it.

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:52.580
Right when we got back
from Thanksgiving break,

00:20:52.580 --> 00:20:54.260
we're driving back from the airport,

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:56.250
she's like, when we get home I'm gonna go

00:20:56.250 --> 00:20:57.740
see a friend of mine.

00:20:57.740 --> 00:20:58.573
Yeah, exactly.

00:20:58.573 --> 00:21:00.893
I'm like, yes, you go girl, you do that.

00:21:00.893 --> 00:21:02.120
It's not just freedom.

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.530
I mean, first of all, it's illogical

00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:06.570
because you are going to need to drive

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:07.710
at some point, right?

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:10.212
And so, the more experience you have.

00:21:10.212 --> 00:21:14.220
Most people are going to need to drive

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:16.800
and the more experience
you have under your belt

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:18.390
the better you will be at things.

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:20.280
It's just like starting something earlier,

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:22.160
when you go back and
like, ah, I wish I had

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:24.650
taken up skiing before I was 25 years old

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:28.720
because you start doing
things that involve

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:32.310
coordination on some level.

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:34.280
The younger you start the better you are

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:35.480
for the rest of your life.

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:37.073
Yeah, like learning Japanese.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:40.203
Not exactly, but similar.

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:43.650
But then, the freedom isn't just for them,

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:46.560
it's for you, the parent as well.

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:48.970
It's mostly about me.

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:51.130
I mean, I think I made
that abundantly clear.

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:52.240
I don't know what it is.

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:56.160
I know that our wives have a
significantly higher tolerance

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:59.993
for just doing things
for the kids than we do,

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:04.750
but I'm just like, Jessie, doesn't it just

00:22:04.750 --> 00:22:07.880
frustrate the pure hell outta you

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:09.740
that you have to stop doing something

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:11.740
and take the kids somewhere?

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:14.270
I mean, for me, I guess I'm more selfish.

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:15.660
I'm just like, no, I don't wanna take you

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:16.580
anywhere right now.

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:17.413
Right.

00:22:17.413 --> 00:22:19.802
I'm doing exactly what I wanna do.

00:22:19.802 --> 00:22:22.610
But, the selfishness
lines up 'cause don't you

00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:23.870
wanna just go?

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:26.310
It's a beautiful thing
when selfishness lines up

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:27.805
as a family.

00:22:27.805 --> 00:22:31.380
I mean, I gotta re-evaluate, man.

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:32.920
I gotta get my wife onboard.

00:22:32.920 --> 00:22:36.370
It did open up another
phenomena because she went

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:38.980
to her friend's house that night and then,

00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:41.133
we're exhausted because
of the time change,

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:45.423
and I texted her and I was
like, when are you comin' back?

00:22:46.550 --> 00:22:48.840
And she was like, I don't know.

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:51.720
And I was like, well, Jade's been confused

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:53.450
'cause you know I went and picked up Jade

00:22:53.450 --> 00:22:55.210
from your house, thank
you for gettin' Jade

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:58.812
from the place, so I wouldn't have to wait

00:22:58.812 --> 00:23:01.670
until the next day to
get Jade from the place,

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:04.463
from the kennel, the non-kennel kennel.

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:06.970
I really appreciate that.

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:10.414
That's bein' a good
friend, gettin' my dog.

00:23:10.414 --> 00:23:11.650
It was pretty easy.

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:14.616
I was like, Jade hasn't
located you so she's

00:23:14.616 --> 00:23:19.340
anxious, she's worried so you
need to come on back home.

00:23:19.340 --> 00:23:20.413
She goes, well, I can
come back in 10 minutes

00:23:20.413 --> 00:23:21.540
or I can come back right now.

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:22.774
It actually worked, but
then I realized I went down

00:23:22.774 --> 00:23:27.510
and I took Jade and we were in her bed

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:30.050
waiting for her to get back,
just sittin' on her bed.

00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:34.990
And so, it was now we've
moved to the waiting up phase

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:40.700
of kiddom and I didn't
think I'd be that guy,

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:42.400
the waiting up for you
to get home before I

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:44.140
go to sleep parent.

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:46.730
But, I kinda did it for
Christy because Christy

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:49.430
was exhausted and she was
like, when's Lilly coming back

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:50.980
and I know that she would
want one of us to stay up,

00:23:50.980 --> 00:23:53.150
so I kinda did it for Christy.

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:54.180
I kinda did it for me, though.

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:56.763
So, I don't know if this is a new thing.

00:23:57.670 --> 00:23:59.563
It doesn't sound selfish, it's weird.

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:02.770
I was waiting up for my child to get home.

00:24:02.770 --> 00:24:03.741
Yeah.

00:24:03.741 --> 00:24:08.467
I do think that there, I
do think we probably apply

00:24:11.210 --> 00:24:14.560
different standards to how much you worry

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:18.650
about Lilly being out
late as a girl versus

00:24:18.650 --> 00:24:20.363
Lock being out late as a guy.

00:24:24.437 --> 00:24:27.620
But, there definitely have
been times when Jessie

00:24:27.620 --> 00:24:31.210
and I have gone to sleep
and he's not back yet.

00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:32.610
But, it's part of the plan.

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:33.710
Yeah.

00:24:33.710 --> 00:24:35.110
Does he wake you up when he comes home?

00:24:35.110 --> 00:24:36.750
He's like hey, I'm, back.

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:39.240
We tend to not be very plan-oriented

00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:40.430
in the McLaughlin household.

00:24:40.430 --> 00:24:42.270
He doesn't have a curfew?

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:45.230
Yeah, he can't stay out until whenever.

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:47.250
We know what he's doing
and where he's going

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:50.423
and I can locate him with
my phone or whatever,

00:24:51.740 --> 00:24:53.830
but it's kinda like I know who you're with

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:56.800
and what you're doing
and there's a general

00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:58.600
sort of expectation at
when you'll be back.

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:00.450
It isn't like oh, it's 1 a.m., I'm back.

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:03.300
And it's not a normal thing.

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:04.740
I'm just saying there have been times

00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:07.386
when we just go to bed
and it's like all right,

00:25:07.386 --> 00:25:08.890
we know where he's at.

00:25:08.890 --> 00:25:10.350
He's coming home, he's
not spending the night

00:25:10.350 --> 00:25:11.580
at this house.

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:13.090
I'm not saying it's wise, I'm just saying

00:25:13.090 --> 00:25:15.298
that we tend to not be real concerned.

00:25:17.160 --> 00:25:18.530
Okay, no judgment.

00:25:18.530 --> 00:25:20.630
I'm just thinkin' I don't know if that's

00:25:20.630 --> 00:25:22.090
where I'm headed next.

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:24.060
We haven't even gotten into
our Thanksgiving stuff,

00:25:24.060 --> 00:25:25.460
so we should do that.

00:25:25.460 --> 00:25:28.120
We will do that in a second, but first

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:30.680
we're going to encourage
you to go to mythical.com

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:33.017
and pick up things that you might like.

00:25:33.017 --> 00:25:35.910
We got that, what are we
callin' the green hoodie?

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:36.743
Green wash.

00:25:36.743 --> 00:25:37.970
Green wash.

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:38.982
Is it like a tie dye thing?

00:25:38.982 --> 00:25:42.100
It's green, black.

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:42.940
It says what on it?

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:44.433
Mythical, GMM.

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:47.350
I mean, I have seen it, there's just a lot

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:48.307
of things over there.

00:25:48.307 --> 00:25:51.410
You know how stuff sells
out, so get on over there

00:25:51.410 --> 00:25:53.270
to mythical.com, check it out.

00:25:53.270 --> 00:25:54.530
Check out all the other stuff.

00:25:54.530 --> 00:25:55.626
And, you know what?

00:25:55.626 --> 00:25:58.510
When we do weird things to sweatshirts

00:25:58.510 --> 00:26:00.264
you guys buy them.

00:26:00.264 --> 00:26:02.420
And so I don't know.

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:04.400
You like weird things done to sweatshirts,

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:05.550
so we'll keep doin' it.

00:26:06.489 --> 00:26:07.690
Maybe.

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:08.670
Until we're tired of it.

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:10.398
Until you're tired of it.

00:26:10.398 --> 00:26:12.760
We'll keep you guessin'
though at the same time.

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:14.290
Mythical.com.

00:26:14.290 --> 00:26:16.840
Okay, let's talk about Thanksgiving.

00:26:16.840 --> 00:26:19.450
We had pretty different Thanksgivings

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:21.420
because you went back to North Carolina,

00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:25.900
saw family and I did not and I actually,

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:28.150
on the complete opposite
end of the spectrum,

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:32.250
I had people at my house
that I'd never met before.

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:33.083
Yeah.

00:26:33.083 --> 00:26:34.170
For Thanksgiving.

00:26:34.170 --> 00:26:35.130
How did that happen?

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:39.330
It was just people walking
by, I invited them in.

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:41.386
You told me you were
gonna fry two turkeys.

00:26:41.386 --> 00:26:43.033
There's a woman that walks
backwards up the hill.

00:26:43.033 --> 00:26:44.460
I was like, come on in and
tell me why you do that,

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:45.340
it's crazy.

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:46.173
Are you?

00:26:46.173 --> 00:26:47.312
What?

00:26:47.312 --> 00:26:49.630
Have you ever seen these
people who walk backwards?

00:26:49.630 --> 00:26:51.033
What are you talkin' about?

00:26:51.033 --> 00:26:53.810
There is a thing that
people do and I think

00:26:53.810 --> 00:26:57.650
that there's probably a story behind it.

00:26:57.650 --> 00:27:00.640
There are more than a few
people who walk backwards.

00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:02.020
Up hills?

00:27:02.020 --> 00:27:03.327
Some people walk backwards in general,

00:27:03.327 --> 00:27:05.530
but people, in fact, there's a person,

00:27:05.530 --> 00:27:10.530
she hasn't done it
recently, but Kelly Oxford,

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:19.440
writer, film maker, she on her Instagram

00:27:20.470 --> 00:27:22.530
used to show this dude who was

00:27:22.530 --> 00:27:23.960
walking backwards everywhere and it was

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:27.110
just flat surface, walking flat.

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:28.800
I haven't seen that, but there's at least

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:30.810
two people in my neighborhood
who walk backwards,

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:32.723
but they're always doing it uphill.

00:27:32.723 --> 00:27:34.910
I don't know if they're like, I feel safe

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:36.850
'cause if I start falling I can catch.

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:39.670
I don't know what it is, but I saw a woman

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:41.010
this morning walking backwards.

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:42.560
There are two people in your neighborhood

00:27:42.560 --> 00:27:43.752
who walk backwards uphill.

00:27:43.752 --> 00:27:44.585
At least two.

00:27:44.585 --> 00:27:46.245
And I don't know if one
started and the other one

00:27:46.245 --> 00:27:47.078
said, this is a good idea.

00:27:47.078 --> 00:27:47.911
I think this is a thing.

00:27:47.911 --> 00:27:50.030
I think there's a philosophy to it,

00:27:50.030 --> 00:27:51.465
to walking backwards uphill.

00:27:51.465 --> 00:27:54.570
I can't come up with what that might be.

00:27:54.570 --> 00:27:57.940
But, it was really just
a joke because I didn't

00:27:57.940 --> 00:27:58.840
invite her in.

00:27:58.840 --> 00:28:00.120
Those weren't the people that I invited.

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:01.710
It was just people who came with friends.

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:06.090
I think it might be a
physiological pain thing,

00:28:06.090 --> 00:28:07.520
like a knee or ankle thing.

00:28:07.520 --> 00:28:10.260
It doesn't hurt my knee
when I walk backwards.

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:11.700
Yeah, I'm sure we can figure it out.

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:12.533
Are they older people?

00:28:12.533 --> 00:28:13.567
But, not now.

00:28:13.567 --> 00:28:16.970
Over 40, which is me, I guess I'm old.

00:28:16.970 --> 00:28:19.890
Well, and you told me that you were frying

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:20.723
two turkeys.

00:28:20.723 --> 00:28:22.047
Three.

00:28:22.047 --> 00:28:22.880
And I was a little worried about that.

00:28:22.880 --> 00:28:23.713
Three?

00:28:23.713 --> 00:28:24.740
Yeah, triple turkey.

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:25.573
Triple turkey.

00:28:25.573 --> 00:28:26.590
Triple turkey.

00:28:26.590 --> 00:28:29.920
So, you must have invited
a lot of strangers

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:31.520
to your house.

00:28:31.520 --> 00:28:33.560
Well, it's a little complicated.

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:36.440
I assume that it's just people Jessie knew

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:37.660
that you didn't know.

00:28:37.660 --> 00:28:38.920
No, no, no, no.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:42.540
There were two people who
neither of us had ever met.

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:43.893
Isn't this what you said last year

00:28:43.893 --> 00:28:46.163
that you didn't wanna do this year?

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:47.903
I probably did.

00:28:47.903 --> 00:28:48.800
I say a lotta things.

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:50.360
You were talking about
Thanksgiving last year.

00:28:50.360 --> 00:28:51.550
I don't like that my kids stay
out whenever they want to.

00:28:51.550 --> 00:28:54.570
Bunch of people and you
were like, you know,

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:56.699
I think I'll do it differently.

00:28:56.699 --> 00:28:58.833
Well, here's what I'll say.

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:01.580
You weren't at that
Thanksgiving last year, right?

00:29:01.580 --> 00:29:03.248
Nope, I was at home.

00:29:03.248 --> 00:29:06.902
I feel like I was playing
cards at a gas station.

00:29:06.902 --> 00:29:08.460
I feel like I can speak more freely

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:10.320
about last year's Thanksgiving now

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:11.820
that we're a year out from it.

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:16.924
So, one of my friends
invited a lot of people

00:29:16.924 --> 00:29:20.360
to a Thanksgiving celebration,
a friendsgiving, if you will,

00:29:20.360 --> 00:29:22.315
but it wasn't really a
friendsgiving because it was

00:29:22.315 --> 00:29:25.300
the majority of the people who were there

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:27.500
I felt like I didn't
know, or at least half.

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:31.660
I can't really remember, but
a lotta people I didn't know

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:33.690
and I didn't really mind it, but the thing

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:37.580
that really was a problem
was that they had gotten

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:41.520
this guy who was a chef
to make all the food

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:44.980
and it was like, the expectations
were really, really high,

00:29:44.980 --> 00:29:48.180
but then he just did a really LA sort

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:49.480
of approach to things.

00:29:49.480 --> 00:29:52.730
He did a dressing that is best described

00:29:52.730 --> 00:29:55.170
as a deconstructed dressing.

00:29:55.170 --> 00:29:57.263
It was kinda like pieces of bread that.

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:00.900
This is all recap.

00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:02.680
I know, but I feel like it's a setup

00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:04.470
for why I did things the
way I did 'em this year.

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:05.396
Okay.

00:30:05.396 --> 00:30:09.020
And I did not, this is all new information

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:12.820
'cause I didn't talk crap about the food.

00:30:12.820 --> 00:30:13.960
I'm doing that a year later.

00:30:13.960 --> 00:30:15.550
Oh.

00:30:15.550 --> 00:30:17.760
There were some vegan friends of mine

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:19.820
who brought a bunch of vegan desserts.

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:21.550
There wasn't even a fricking pumpkin pie

00:30:21.550 --> 00:30:23.640
that wasn't, there was no butter in any

00:30:23.640 --> 00:30:24.473
of the desserts.

00:30:24.473 --> 00:30:28.480
It's just like, listen
y'all, Thanksgiving,

00:30:28.480 --> 00:30:31.320
if just a couple of times
a year you're gonna let

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:34.590
it all hang out, let it all hang out.

00:30:34.590 --> 00:30:38.310
Unzip it, unbutton it, unfurl it.

00:30:38.310 --> 00:30:40.150
I get it, if you're a vegan you're not

00:30:40.150 --> 00:30:44.583
gonna put butter in something,
but if you're not a vegan,

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:48.257
put butter in it and put a lot of butter

00:30:48.257 --> 00:30:51.460
and a lot of sugar and
get it to taste good.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.130
Let that be the goal.

00:30:53.130 --> 00:30:55.973
So, you were focused on a traditional,

00:30:56.854 --> 00:30:58.883
sugar and butter first, menu.

00:30:59.986 --> 00:31:01.132
There was so much butter
and so much sugar.

00:31:01.132 --> 00:31:02.540
And fried turkey.

00:31:02.540 --> 00:31:03.373
And a lotta meat.

00:31:03.373 --> 00:31:07.200
So, yeah, I deep fried three turkeys

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:08.670
and the reason I did three turkeys is

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:13.670
because when our friend, Jenny, found out

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:17.470
that I was deep frying turkeys,

00:31:17.470 --> 00:31:22.100
she said, do you mind if I bring by

00:31:22.100 --> 00:31:25.020
just a turkey breast and
you can fry that, too,

00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:26.223
for my leftovers?

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:30.396
I love her.

00:31:30.396 --> 00:31:32.620
And I was like, of course, I don't mind.

00:31:32.620 --> 00:31:34.350
Okay, so you're counting her turkey breast

00:31:34.350 --> 00:31:37.020
as, you got the vat, it's
hot, you might as well

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:37.853
dunk another.

00:31:37.853 --> 00:31:39.290
Right, it was like, and I was almost like,

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:40.123
you know what?

00:31:40.123 --> 00:31:41.600
I'll start with your turkey breast

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:43.200
because that'll be, it's a little bit

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:44.033
of a guinea pig.

00:31:44.033 --> 00:31:45.360
It wasn't a guinea pig, it was a turkey,

00:31:45.360 --> 00:31:48.766
but it would be like my test turkey.

00:31:48.766 --> 00:31:50.770
And she was funny 'cause she was like,

00:31:50.770 --> 00:31:55.490
my dad has this tradition
of completely cooking

00:31:55.490 --> 00:31:59.110
a separate turkey breast
and that is your leftovers,

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:02.520
so there's not a lot of
pressure on the main turkey

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:03.778
to be left over.

00:32:03.778 --> 00:32:05.133
Eat it all.

00:32:06.337 --> 00:32:09.147
Then you have this completely
like prime turkey breast

00:32:09.147 --> 00:32:10.990
that's ready to go.

00:32:10.990 --> 00:32:13.147
And she was like, and can
you put this poultry magic

00:32:13.147 --> 00:32:14.860
rub on it?

00:32:14.860 --> 00:32:15.693
She gave me the rub.

00:32:15.693 --> 00:32:17.590
She brought me the turkey
breast and the rub.

00:32:17.590 --> 00:32:19.040
I was like, sure, no problem.

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:22.870
But, with the other two, and she got,

00:32:22.870 --> 00:32:24.300
it was like a Butterball turkey.

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:27.080
It was a turkey that
was nice and packaged,

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:28.340
turkey breast nice and packaged.

00:32:28.340 --> 00:32:29.700
It comes with, it's already got

00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:30.968
a sodium and sugar solution in it,

00:32:30.968 --> 00:32:32.863
so you don't need to brine it.

00:32:34.090 --> 00:32:39.080
But of course, my wife gets

00:32:41.282 --> 00:32:46.282
the Heritage Farm organic
never frozen turkey

00:32:48.590 --> 00:32:52.760
that's been, this turkey's been massaged.

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:55.010
This turkey's probably
taken calculus classes

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:56.420
to get ready for this moment

00:32:57.290 --> 00:33:02.290
and these turkeys were like $85 a piece

00:33:03.080 --> 00:33:07.470
for a 13 pound turkey, just to
put things into perspective.

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:08.997
These were some expensive assed turkeys.

00:33:08.997 --> 00:33:12.320
And it wasn't a bigger turkey
'cause the well treated

00:33:12.320 --> 00:33:13.470
turkeys are smaller.

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:16.482
Right, they don't have those
giant antibiotic breasts.

00:33:16.482 --> 00:33:19.893
They're just like pulsating,
ready to be eaten.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:23.500
These are like a little more natural.

00:33:23.500 --> 00:33:26.240
These turkeys have,
they've run around the yard

00:33:26.240 --> 00:33:27.073
a little bit.

00:33:28.248 --> 00:33:29.930
So, I was like, I gotta get this right.

00:33:29.930 --> 00:33:30.847
These are some expensive turkeys.

00:33:30.847 --> 00:33:34.330
And I did two turkeys
because I did the whole,

00:33:34.330 --> 00:33:36.521
you roughly do a pound per person

00:33:36.521 --> 00:33:38.820
and you're gonna get leftovers with that

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:39.653
and I was like, you know what?

00:33:39.653 --> 00:33:41.530
I want a lotta leftovers and I want 'em,

00:33:41.530 --> 00:33:43.410
if one turkey turns out
better than the other,

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:44.690
give me two turkeys.

00:33:44.690 --> 00:33:49.650
So, I did the brining.

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:52.040
I brined it two days ahead of time.

00:33:52.040 --> 00:33:55.510
I did the rub and I did all this homemade

00:33:55.510 --> 00:33:57.970
and I followed the Pioneer Woman's recipe.

00:33:57.970 --> 00:33:59.970
Me and the Pioneer Woman are like this.

00:33:59.970 --> 00:34:01.260
Pioneer Woman.

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:02.430
She did a television show, didn't she?

00:34:02.430 --> 00:34:06.130
Yeah, and so I followed her brining recipe

00:34:06.130 --> 00:34:11.100
and her rubbing recipe
and then I injected.

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:13.120
I had my way with these turkeys.

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:16.240
I rubbed 'em, I injected
'em, I brined 'em.

00:34:16.240 --> 00:34:17.650
I'm beginning to
understand why you invited

00:34:17.650 --> 00:34:18.483
strangers over.

00:34:18.483 --> 00:34:21.743
I spent way too much
time with those turkeys.

00:34:21.743 --> 00:34:25.210
You can get away with more bragging

00:34:25.210 --> 00:34:27.190
when people don't know you that well,

00:34:27.190 --> 00:34:28.870
they'll sit and listen to
you brag about what you did

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:30.640
to your turkeys for hours.

00:34:30.640 --> 00:34:32.480
No, I actually had a lot
of self-deprecating talk

00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:34.220
about the turkeys because
I was nervous about 'em.

00:34:34.220 --> 00:34:36.360
Because, what is the one
thing that everyone's

00:34:36.360 --> 00:34:37.360
worried about with turkey?

00:34:37.360 --> 00:34:38.996
What is the one thing that
people like you ham men

00:34:38.996 --> 00:34:40.830
complain about?

00:34:40.830 --> 00:34:41.840
You don't want a dry turkey.

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:42.840
It's too dry.

00:34:42.840 --> 00:34:46.620
And, my theory is that
it isn't that people

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:47.453
don't like turkey.

00:34:47.453 --> 00:34:49.560
It's that people don't like dry turkey

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:53.240
and that most people and a lot of people

00:34:53.240 --> 00:34:56.600
who do not like turkey have been deprived

00:34:56.600 --> 00:34:59.150
of adequately moist turkey and therefore,

00:34:59.150 --> 00:35:03.410
they've come to wrong
conclusions about turkeys.

00:35:03.410 --> 00:35:04.960
It isn't actually dry if you do it right.

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:09.510
So, I'm deep frying these things.

00:35:09.510 --> 00:35:10.950
I haven't done that in like 15 years,

00:35:10.950 --> 00:35:12.890
one time Chris and I did
it back in North Carolina,

00:35:12.890 --> 00:35:13.723
but it was different.

00:35:13.723 --> 00:35:16.880
I got the electric fryer, so I didn't do

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:18.028
the whole gas situation.

00:35:18.028 --> 00:35:20.960
I read the statistic
that 4300 people a year

00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:22.900
burn their homes down
frying, deep frying turkeys,

00:35:22.900 --> 00:35:24.570
which sounds like a
made up stat, but I saw

00:35:24.570 --> 00:35:27.520
it on many websites, so it must be true.

00:35:27.520 --> 00:35:29.584
So, this is basically
like a giant deep fryer

00:35:29.584 --> 00:35:32.760
that has a lid and everything.

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:36.370
Got the oil, peanut oil, put it in there.

00:35:36.370 --> 00:35:38.750
It's all about gettin' the
right level on that oil.

00:35:38.750 --> 00:35:39.950
Well, they make it easy.

00:35:39.950 --> 00:35:42.500
There's a min line and a max line

00:35:42.500 --> 00:35:45.080
and it basically said if your turkey

00:35:45.080 --> 00:35:47.670
is a certain size or certain pounds,

00:35:47.670 --> 00:35:49.360
if you put it at the max
line it'll be covered,

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:50.943
so I did that and it was fine.

00:35:52.600 --> 00:35:54.360
Finished that first turkey.

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:55.553
It looked great.

00:35:57.160 --> 00:35:58.610
Did the second one and the third one.

00:35:58.610 --> 00:36:01.248
It was not easy to hold,
get the temperature

00:36:01.248 --> 00:36:04.040
anyway, I was a little bit worried

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:06.140
'cause I thought I went a little too long.

00:36:07.470 --> 00:36:10.660
But, boy the turkey talk started as soon

00:36:10.660 --> 00:36:13.310
as people started biting
into those breasts.

00:36:13.310 --> 00:36:16.380
And of course, I had, I did dark meat

00:36:16.380 --> 00:36:18.329
and white meat on the platter,
dark meat on one side,

00:36:18.329 --> 00:36:19.987
white meat and I did the whole thing.

00:36:19.987 --> 00:36:23.820
Me and Nick, we sat
there and we trimmed it.

00:36:23.820 --> 00:36:24.900
We took all the meat off.

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:28.106
Do you have an electric cutter?

00:36:28.106 --> 00:36:30.800
Jenny, the way that she
paid for her turkey is

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:32.540
she let me use her electric knife.

00:36:32.540 --> 00:36:34.380
And so, I cut it up.

00:36:34.380 --> 00:36:37.890
It had a little bit of the
brine flavor was coming through

00:36:37.890 --> 00:36:39.270
'cause I put some orange peel in there,

00:36:39.270 --> 00:36:41.230
only because the Pioneer
Woman wanted me to

00:36:41.230 --> 00:36:42.933
and I do whatever she says.

00:36:43.782 --> 00:36:44.615
She'd strangle a wolf.

00:36:44.615 --> 00:36:49.610
Yeah, all I can say is
the turkey was a success

00:36:50.950 --> 00:36:55.210
and I'm very proud of myself.

00:36:55.210 --> 00:36:58.080
I mean, it was the moistest breast

00:36:58.080 --> 00:36:59.630
this side of the Mississippi.

00:36:59.630 --> 00:37:01.240
I'm sure east of the Mississippi there was

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:03.290
some very moist breasts because the people

00:37:03.290 --> 00:37:04.870
in the south can really cook.

00:37:04.870 --> 00:37:07.380
The Pioneer Woman is west
of the Mississippi as well.

00:37:07.380 --> 00:37:09.930
Is she on the Plains?

00:37:09.930 --> 00:37:11.529
Yeah, she's cookin' up a turkey this year.

00:37:11.529 --> 00:37:15.730
And Jessie was like, well,
you're in trouble now

00:37:15.730 --> 00:37:17.770
because you've gotta do that again.

00:37:17.770 --> 00:37:20.060
You have to do this every year.

00:37:20.060 --> 00:37:23.270
That we're here because
you've shown that you can.

00:37:23.270 --> 00:37:25.050
Like living, like above the ground.

00:37:25.050 --> 00:37:27.840
The problem, it took so much time.

00:37:27.840 --> 00:37:30.560
In fact, I actually had this whole idea

00:37:30.560 --> 00:37:33.110
that I was going to
Instagram this process.

00:37:33.110 --> 00:37:38.110
I had a whole character, Terry
and his Turkey Tutorials,

00:37:38.270 --> 00:37:39.103
I had a whole character.

00:37:39.103 --> 00:37:40.760
I had a pair of glasses,

00:37:40.760 --> 00:37:42.160
a whole thing that I was gonna do.

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:44.590
And as soon as I got into
looking at the directions,

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:47.600
I was like, oh gosh, I
don't have the ability

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:49.930
to be entertaining and do this right.

00:37:49.930 --> 00:37:52.550
Maybe next year, Terry
and his Turkey Tutorials

00:37:52.550 --> 00:37:55.750
will be back, will be
there for the first time,

00:37:55.750 --> 00:37:57.450
because now I understand
what I'm up against.

00:37:57.450 --> 00:38:00.285
Turkey Terry had to tarry until 2020.

00:38:00.285 --> 00:38:02.500
But, it was a success.

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:07.050
But, the phenomenon of,
and just so you know,

00:38:07.050 --> 00:38:09.160
I'm gonna talk about my Thanksgiving

00:38:09.160 --> 00:38:10.430
and then I'm gonna hand it over to Link

00:38:10.430 --> 00:38:12.030
and he's gonna talk all
about his Thanksgiving,

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:14.386
so I'm not intentionally
hogging the conversation.

00:38:14.386 --> 00:38:16.510
We have a plan.

00:38:16.510 --> 00:38:18.246
Don't bring a hog into this.

00:38:18.246 --> 00:38:19.079
Keep your turkey.

00:38:19.079 --> 00:38:21.310
You did have a hog on your
Thanksgiving, I assume.

00:38:22.886 --> 00:38:25.300
But, you didn't have, man I should've kept

00:38:25.300 --> 00:38:26.390
some of that breast behind for you

00:38:26.390 --> 00:38:27.970
so you could see how moist it is.

00:38:27.970 --> 00:38:31.190
But anyway, having people.

00:38:31.190 --> 00:38:32.023
I'm a ham man.

00:38:32.023 --> 00:38:34.290
I wasn't particularly, I like to save

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:35.123
the ham for Christmas.

00:38:35.123 --> 00:38:35.956
That's the thing.

00:38:35.956 --> 00:38:39.880
I'm a Christmas ham and
they're so close together,

00:38:39.880 --> 00:38:41.500
the holidays are so close together,

00:38:41.500 --> 00:38:45.165
they're like a month apart,
so I feel like ham, ham.

00:38:45.165 --> 00:38:47.670
Can a ham man have too much ham?

00:38:47.670 --> 00:38:48.503
I feel like they can.

00:38:48.503 --> 00:38:50.810
I don't.

00:38:50.810 --> 00:38:54.460
I wasn't super excited about having people

00:38:54.460 --> 00:38:57.020
that I didn't know because I tend to be

00:38:57.020 --> 00:38:59.840
a hermit and a loner and I'm the kinda guy

00:38:59.840 --> 00:39:01.090
that when there's a bunch
of people in my house

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:02.630
I go upstairs for a little bit.

00:39:02.630 --> 00:39:04.028
I'm that guy.

00:39:04.028 --> 00:39:07.280
But my wife is my wife, as you know,

00:39:07.280 --> 00:39:10.020
and she's the most welcoming
and hospitable person

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:12.370
on the face of the planet and will talk

00:39:12.370 --> 00:39:16.033
to a stranger into like 4 a.m.

00:39:17.850 --> 00:39:20.993
And so, I was like, no, this is good.

00:39:24.667 --> 00:39:25.500
This, meaning what?

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:26.660
Having people that I don't know.

00:39:26.660 --> 00:39:27.994
It's great.

00:39:27.994 --> 00:39:29.314
Well, how do people
you don't know show up?

00:39:29.314 --> 00:39:30.880
They knew people that I do know.

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:32.817
So okay, it's like bring other people.

00:39:32.817 --> 00:39:34.101
It's like, hey.

00:39:34.101 --> 00:39:36.360
We're in LA.

00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:39.050
You invite one, you invite this one family

00:39:39.050 --> 00:39:40.800
and they're like, we
were gonna get together

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:42.560
with so and so, can we bring them?

00:39:42.560 --> 00:39:44.362
Oh, and she was gonna bring so and so

00:39:44.362 --> 00:39:46.180
and he might bring so and so.

00:39:46.180 --> 00:39:47.551
It's the chain effect.

00:39:47.551 --> 00:39:48.384
Yeah.

00:39:48.384 --> 00:39:49.423
And we were like, sure.

00:39:49.423 --> 00:39:51.643
Must have been 18 people, whatever.

00:39:53.590 --> 00:39:54.423
But, it was great.

00:39:54.423 --> 00:39:57.955
And the thing that, it
was nice getting to know

00:39:57.955 --> 00:40:02.930
new people, but it also
created this dynamic

00:40:02.930 --> 00:40:07.930
where we had not planned
on, what are we doing?

00:40:10.170 --> 00:40:12.450
We knew what time we were
gonna eat approximate.

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:14.853
Of course, we ate later than that.

00:40:15.803 --> 00:40:17.110
We were eatin' kinda late.

00:40:17.110 --> 00:40:18.720
We were eating at 6 o'clock.

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:21.720
So all right, what about
Thanksgiving night?

00:40:21.720 --> 00:40:23.429
How long are people gonna stay?

00:40:23.429 --> 00:40:27.346
People with kids kinda started
peelin' off, peelin' out.

00:40:27.346 --> 00:40:32.346
And then, a few people
stayed and we were like,

00:40:33.430 --> 00:40:34.830
let's watch a movie.

00:40:34.830 --> 00:40:37.090
And I don't know who suggested it.

00:40:37.090 --> 00:40:40.455
Somehow we decided that we
were gonna watch Footloose.

00:40:40.455 --> 00:40:42.680
Footloose.

00:40:42.680 --> 00:40:47.680
Footloose, Kevin Bacon, 1984, I guess.

00:40:48.340 --> 00:40:49.490
Have you seen that?

00:40:49.490 --> 00:40:50.723
I'm an American, yes.

00:40:52.900 --> 00:40:55.445
I have not seen that.

00:40:55.445 --> 00:40:57.300
Sounds painful.

00:40:57.300 --> 00:40:58.700
It's like your foot's loose.

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:02.180
Is this a medical drama?

00:41:02.180 --> 00:41:03.950
God, my foot's loose.

00:41:03.950 --> 00:41:06.710
Must be with the ankle, I don't know.

00:41:06.710 --> 00:41:10.290
I haven't seen Footloose since that wave

00:41:10.290 --> 00:41:13.173
in the '80s where most people
were watching Footloose,

00:41:14.060 --> 00:41:17.361
so I kinda knew, I've seen it.

00:41:17.361 --> 00:41:21.200
I know about the famous
speech to the City Council

00:41:21.200 --> 00:41:23.190
and stuff like that
that you see on YouTube

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:24.023
and that kinda thing.

00:41:24.023 --> 00:41:25.410
And of course, the dances.

00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:28.260
I know about the dances in socks.

00:41:28.260 --> 00:41:30.460
That's really at the beginning.

00:41:30.460 --> 00:41:34.383
In fact, most of that close foot action.

00:41:35.420 --> 00:41:37.317
Or is that Tom, is Tom Cruz in that?

00:41:37.317 --> 00:41:39.330
No, you're thinkin' about
Risky Business probably

00:41:39.330 --> 00:41:40.830
where he's dancing with just a shirt.

00:41:40.830 --> 00:41:43.350
Do you know that John Lithgow is in,

00:41:43.350 --> 00:41:44.593
he plays the pastor.

00:41:45.440 --> 00:41:48.440
Because the story of
Footloose is that this

00:41:50.350 --> 00:41:53.840
sort of super conservative
Midwestern town,

00:41:53.840 --> 00:41:55.560
which I actually thought
was really interesting

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:57.923
in light of Bleak Creek, and this story,

00:41:57.923 --> 00:42:00.480
I didn't think about Footloose one time

00:42:00.480 --> 00:42:01.650
when we were writing Bleak Creek.

00:42:01.650 --> 00:42:03.810
You know I didn't.

00:42:03.810 --> 00:42:08.640
Because you've got this
super conservative town

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:10.270
where the pastor has a lotta control.

00:42:10.270 --> 00:42:11.170
He won't let 'em dance?

00:42:11.170 --> 00:42:12.540
And you can't dance.

00:42:12.540 --> 00:42:13.373
Oh.

00:42:13.373 --> 00:42:15.310
And Kevin Bacon comes in, new kid

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:18.270
from outta town and of
course, dancin' is his thing.

00:42:18.270 --> 00:42:21.663
And it's a story of him tryin'
to get the town to dance.

00:42:22.830 --> 00:42:24.250
That's a Thanksgiving movie.

00:42:24.250 --> 00:42:28.390
Now, it isn't a Thanksgiving movie, right,

00:42:28.390 --> 00:42:32.100
but I discovered something
that I've never heard

00:42:32.100 --> 00:42:33.490
anybody point out.

00:42:33.490 --> 00:42:36.760
I almost tweeted it, but then, that takes

00:42:36.760 --> 00:42:37.620
a little more effort.

00:42:37.620 --> 00:42:39.570
Turkey Terry didn't feel like tweeting.

00:42:43.772 --> 00:42:47.090
At the end of the movie, spoiler alert,

00:42:47.090 --> 00:42:48.093
there's a dance.

00:42:49.260 --> 00:42:50.853
The town gets to dance.

00:42:53.190 --> 00:42:56.650
At the dance the camera is moving across

00:42:56.650 --> 00:43:00.020
the spread of food that's at the dance

00:43:00.020 --> 00:43:03.040
and the food at the dance is unlike

00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:05.490
any food at any dance there has ever been

00:43:05.490 --> 00:43:09.810
'cause it's like whole
baked pies and cakes.

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:10.800
Thanksgiving.

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:13.660
And then, as the camera tilts up

00:43:13.660 --> 00:43:15.120
and you see in the background at the end

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:17.520
of the table, a turkey.

00:43:17.520 --> 00:43:20.260
There is a turkey on the table.

00:43:20.260 --> 00:43:21.093
It's Thanksgiving.

00:43:21.093 --> 00:43:23.620
It's a Thanksgiving movie,
but it's not Thanksgiving.

00:43:23.620 --> 00:43:25.900
It's just their dance.

00:43:25.900 --> 00:43:29.000
And I was like, has anybody
ever noticed this before?

00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:30.110
There's a turkey.

00:43:30.110 --> 00:43:32.490
And then, I thought about
pausing it and rewinding

00:43:32.490 --> 00:43:34.080
it to make sure that it was a turkey,

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:35.780
but then I was like, what if I rewind

00:43:35.780 --> 00:43:37.210
and it's not a turkey?

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:38.970
That'll ruin it for me.

00:43:38.970 --> 00:43:40.330
Plus, there's strangers watching.

00:43:40.330 --> 00:43:41.163
Turkey Terry.

00:43:41.163 --> 00:43:44.120
Who's this guy pausing
the movie at the end

00:43:44.120 --> 00:43:45.140
and rewinding it?

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:47.170
It was funny though, 'cause
it's one of those things,

00:43:47.170 --> 00:43:48.660
it's one of those things that happens

00:43:48.660 --> 00:43:50.060
that feels like a glitch in the matrix

00:43:50.060 --> 00:43:51.800
because Turkey Terry
has spent all this time

00:43:51.800 --> 00:43:53.333
on the turkeys, getting
the turkeys just right.

00:43:53.333 --> 00:43:54.910
He's seein' turkeys everywhere.

00:43:54.910 --> 00:43:56.280
And then, all of a sudden
he's watchin' Footloose

00:43:56.280 --> 00:43:57.670
and there's a turkey on the table.

00:43:57.670 --> 00:43:59.020
What are the chances?

00:43:59.020 --> 00:44:00.360
This is a glitch in the matrix.

00:44:00.360 --> 00:44:01.193
100%.

00:44:01.193 --> 00:44:02.950
And I want that glitch to remain

00:44:02.950 --> 00:44:05.096
and I do think that it's true

00:44:05.096 --> 00:44:06.559
that there is a turkey on the table.

00:44:06.559 --> 00:44:09.367
There's pies on the table
and there's a large, brown

00:44:09.367 --> 00:44:12.660
baked bird at the end of the table.

00:44:12.660 --> 00:44:13.493
It's Thanksgiving.

00:44:13.493 --> 00:44:16.010
So, Footloose is a Thanksgiving movie

00:44:16.010 --> 00:44:18.260
and you know what?

00:44:18.260 --> 00:44:20.133
Shepherd watched it with me,

00:44:21.099 --> 00:44:23.137
watched it with us 'cause Jessie and I

00:44:23.137 --> 00:44:24.637
and then whoever else was left

00:44:25.667 --> 00:44:26.617
and he was into it.

00:44:28.363 --> 00:44:29.450
We learned things.

00:44:29.450 --> 00:44:33.210
Kevin Bacon, Kevin Bacon
has really held up.

00:44:33.210 --> 00:44:34.762
You know the thing that you do now

00:44:34.762 --> 00:44:36.620
when you watch movies and you're looking

00:44:36.620 --> 00:44:38.430
at an actor and you're like, it's 1984,

00:44:38.430 --> 00:44:39.390
how old was he then?

00:44:39.390 --> 00:44:40.460
How old is he now?

00:44:40.460 --> 00:44:43.110
And then you start looking
at modern pictures of people.

00:44:43.110 --> 00:44:43.943
Same.

00:44:45.197 --> 00:44:46.727
You start seeing how
the actors in the movie

00:44:46.727 --> 00:44:49.390
have aged and you find
out, oh, Chris Penn,

00:44:49.390 --> 00:44:52.020
Sean Penn's brother, who
plays his friend, he's dead.

00:44:52.020 --> 00:44:53.731
He died in 2006.

00:44:53.731 --> 00:44:55.123
Oh, that's sad.

00:44:55.123 --> 00:44:57.030
And then, you're like what
does Kevin Bacon look like?

00:44:57.030 --> 00:44:59.010
Whoa, Kevin Bacon looks good.

00:44:59.010 --> 00:45:00.742
What has Kevin Bacon been
doing to look so good?

00:45:00.742 --> 00:45:02.640
Not eatin' bacon, I'll tell you that.

00:45:02.640 --> 00:45:04.160
Dancin'.

00:45:04.160 --> 00:45:05.666
Dancin'.

00:45:05.666 --> 00:45:07.090
So, and then that.

00:45:07.090 --> 00:45:08.490
Do you wanna hear about my Thanksgiving?

00:45:08.490 --> 00:45:10.010
Yeah, the only, the last thing I'll say is

00:45:10.010 --> 00:45:11.920
that watching a movie
on Thanksgiving night

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:13.850
opened up the floodgates
and ended up seeing

00:45:13.850 --> 00:45:15.840
more movies in a shorter period of time.

00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:18.130
We went to the theater twice,

00:45:18.130 --> 00:45:20.600
saw Knives Out, highly recommend it.

00:45:20.600 --> 00:45:22.930
And then, I already told you about this,

00:45:22.930 --> 00:45:26.300
saw Ford vs. Ferrari, which is the ratio

00:45:26.300 --> 00:45:27.990
of how much I wanted to see a movie

00:45:27.990 --> 00:45:29.660
to how good it was.

00:45:29.660 --> 00:45:31.600
I didn't want to see that movie at all.

00:45:31.600 --> 00:45:33.317
It's like a racing movie, I don't care.

00:45:33.317 --> 00:45:37.393
Ford vs. Ferrari, you gotta see it.

00:45:38.240 --> 00:45:39.580
It's a great movie.

00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:40.413
Don't make that face.

00:45:40.413 --> 00:45:41.490
Have you seen it?

00:45:41.490 --> 00:45:44.730
You need to see it because
of course, it's got

00:45:44.730 --> 00:45:45.870
great actors in it, but it's just

00:45:45.870 --> 00:45:47.410
a really compelling story.

00:45:47.410 --> 00:45:49.310
My kids loved it.

00:45:49.310 --> 00:45:50.550
I loved it.

00:45:50.550 --> 00:45:52.815
Jo Jo Rabbit is the best
movie of the year so far.

00:45:52.815 --> 00:45:53.648
We already talked about that.

00:45:53.648 --> 00:45:55.700
But, I saw that before Thanksgiving.

00:45:55.700 --> 00:45:57.330
What about your Thanksgiving, Neil?

00:45:57.330 --> 00:45:59.940
I didn't have, I didn't have a turkey.

00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:03.740
I mean, I'm tryin' not to, you know what?

00:46:03.740 --> 00:46:05.340
I'm gonna try and make this introspective

00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:08.760
and not a downer, but I think the spoiler

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:11.490
is some downer elements
to my Thanksgiving.

00:46:11.490 --> 00:46:14.736
So, it's not bright eyed and bushy tailed

00:46:14.736 --> 00:46:18.930
like your Footloose
Turkey Terry shenanigans.

00:46:18.930 --> 00:46:20.358
Footloose and fancy free.

00:46:20.358 --> 00:46:25.358
We didn't have, you know,
we went to my nanny's house.

00:46:27.010 --> 00:46:31.360
It's like, she and Aunt
Vickie, we didn't wanna burden

00:46:31.360 --> 00:46:34.094
them and have 'em make food.

00:46:34.094 --> 00:46:39.094
So Nanny, she's not able to
make her signature dressing

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:41.583
that, that dressing that we would get

00:46:41.583 --> 00:46:44.510
that like, slather that
gravy on it every year.

00:46:44.510 --> 00:46:45.343
I grew up eatin' that.

00:46:45.343 --> 00:46:49.027
It was like the perfect
Thanksgiving dressing.

00:46:49.027 --> 00:46:50.727
Has she written the recipe down?

00:46:52.100 --> 00:46:54.120
'Cause that's how, you gotta
hold on to those recipes.

00:46:54.120 --> 00:46:54.953
That's a good point.

00:46:54.953 --> 00:46:56.390
Recipes from your relatives,

00:46:56.390 --> 00:46:59.450
it's really, really important
that they write those down.

00:46:59.450 --> 00:47:00.720
That's one of the ways that we remember

00:47:00.720 --> 00:47:03.290
Jessie's grandmother is make those cakes

00:47:03.290 --> 00:47:04.510
that she used to make.

00:47:04.510 --> 00:47:05.520
Yeah, that's a good point

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:08.880
because she's not able to
make that and we didn't

00:47:08.880 --> 00:47:11.030
wanna burden them to have to make stuff,

00:47:11.030 --> 00:47:13.373
so we brought in Bojangles.

00:47:15.288 --> 00:47:16.182
That's a good Thanksgiving.

00:47:16.182 --> 00:47:19.810
My kids were just so excited.

00:47:19.810 --> 00:47:21.610
'Cause they knew we had planned this.

00:47:21.610 --> 00:47:23.890
We're gonna get Bojangles for Thanksgiving

00:47:23.890 --> 00:47:24.860
at Nanny's house.

00:47:24.860 --> 00:47:28.680
And so, that's quite a silver lining.

00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:30.482
I mean, it's not a Thanksgiving meal,

00:47:30.482 --> 00:47:35.482
but it's Bojangles and we never get that.

00:47:35.700 --> 00:47:37.260
So, it was pretty great.

00:47:37.260 --> 00:47:42.260
But, they're not doing well, so it's like,

00:47:42.580 --> 00:47:44.130
and we're talkin' to Nanny about,

00:47:44.130 --> 00:47:47.533
she's like, I gotta pull my will together.

00:47:48.700 --> 00:47:50.330
That's fun table talk.

00:47:50.330 --> 00:47:51.390
So, here we are on Thanksgiving.

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:54.330
We're tryin' to, we're
talkin' about the finer points

00:47:54.330 --> 00:47:57.650
of the will, which is
a very important thing

00:47:57.650 --> 00:48:00.480
to talk about and we had been
talkin' about it leading up

00:48:00.480 --> 00:48:03.180
to Thanksgiving, but
since we were together

00:48:03.180 --> 00:48:04.957
in the same room we were
talkin' about it a little bit.

00:48:04.957 --> 00:48:06.980
So, it tends to be a
bit of a downer talkin'

00:48:06.980 --> 00:48:09.780
about a will, but it's
also very important.

00:48:09.780 --> 00:48:11.760
Unless you're a lawyer.

00:48:11.760 --> 00:48:13.300
Yeah,

00:48:13.300 --> 00:48:17.670
So, that was kinda the vibe this year.

00:48:17.670 --> 00:48:20.571
There was no going to a gas
station and playing cards.

00:48:20.571 --> 00:48:22.950
We had planned things out a little better,

00:48:22.950 --> 00:48:25.720
but also mom and Lewis
weren't able to show up

00:48:25.720 --> 00:48:30.210
because of Lewis's health
and actually the last time

00:48:30.210 --> 00:48:34.290
I talked about it on Ear Biscuits, I guess

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:35.800
it was the whole episode devoted

00:48:35.800 --> 00:48:39.810
to their visit out here and how he

00:48:39.810 --> 00:48:42.690
basically almost died, right.

00:48:42.690 --> 00:48:46.240
And then, he recovered well enough

00:48:46.240 --> 00:48:48.520
that they were able to fly back home

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:50.830
and then I remember tellin the story

00:48:50.830 --> 00:48:53.404
that when he got home he was blowin' off

00:48:53.404 --> 00:48:56.620
his garage with the leaf blower.

00:48:56.620 --> 00:48:58.440
Good, good, good.

00:48:58.440 --> 00:49:01.250
Well, I haven't talked
about it since then,

00:49:01.250 --> 00:49:06.250
but the update there is a
few days after that he had

00:49:08.210 --> 00:49:09.920
a series of strokes.

00:49:09.920 --> 00:49:12.400
You know about this,
but they don't, so I'll

00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:14.410
just kinda bring 'em up to speed.

00:49:14.410 --> 00:49:17.830
And again, that was back in April.

00:49:17.830 --> 00:49:22.140
So, ever since April he's been in and out

00:49:22.140 --> 00:49:25.670
of the hospital and rehab facilities

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:30.670
in order to regain the ability to walk

00:49:30.697 --> 00:49:35.613
and gain the strength to do things.

00:49:37.260 --> 00:49:41.650
And he's, it's been very difficult,

00:49:41.650 --> 00:49:44.700
but right before we came back home

00:49:44.700 --> 00:49:46.960
for Thanksgiving was the first time

00:49:46.960 --> 00:49:49.440
that he came back home since April.

00:49:49.440 --> 00:49:52.129
He's been in and out of
facilities this whole time.

00:49:52.129 --> 00:49:52.962
Just when you were home?

00:49:52.962 --> 00:49:53.795
Yeah.

00:49:53.795 --> 00:49:56.973
I mean, back in July when we went home,

00:49:56.973 --> 00:50:00.380
when we were filming at Buies Creek

00:50:00.380 --> 00:50:04.810
for the Bleak Creek
conversation documentary

00:50:04.810 --> 00:50:08.480
that is now on Mythical
Morning, we would film

00:50:09.775 --> 00:50:10.608
there in the day and
then every night I would

00:50:11.458 --> 00:50:12.735
go visit mom and Lewis in
the hospital and he was,

00:50:12.735 --> 00:50:15.973
he was basically, he was on a ventilator.

00:50:17.001 --> 00:50:19.780
Ventilator may not be the right word.

00:50:19.780 --> 00:50:24.570
He had a feeding tube
and he was not conscious.

00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:25.710
It was bad.

00:50:25.710 --> 00:50:30.710
He had four or five
near death experiences,

00:50:30.750 --> 00:50:34.380
once leaving LA since April.

00:50:34.380 --> 00:50:37.997
And so, this was the first
time seein' him in person

00:50:37.997 --> 00:50:40.850
and he finally has gotten back home,

00:50:40.850 --> 00:50:42.550
which is great news.

00:50:42.550 --> 00:50:46.420
So, he is on a road to recovery,

00:50:46.420 --> 00:50:48.650
but they weren't able to
come to Nanny's house.

00:50:48.650 --> 00:50:51.990
We had Thanksgiving, Bojangles at Nanny's

00:50:51.990 --> 00:50:56.070
and then later that
afternoon we went to Mom's

00:50:56.070 --> 00:50:59.410
and saw Lewis and he'd lost 65 pounds.

00:50:59.410 --> 00:51:02.110
He looks like a totally different person.

00:51:02.110 --> 00:51:02.943
Yeah.

00:51:02.943 --> 00:51:05.420
You know, it's like, I mean, he looked

00:51:05.420 --> 00:51:07.127
a whole lot better than
he did when I saw him

00:51:07.127 --> 00:51:10.620
all swollen and in the
hospital every night

00:51:10.620 --> 00:51:12.160
when I'd visit them in July.

00:51:12.160 --> 00:51:17.160
So, in a way it was difficult,
but it was also very good

00:51:19.748 --> 00:51:24.570
to see him at home and know
that he had just been there

00:51:24.570 --> 00:51:28.140
a few days and they were
very thrilled to be there.

00:51:28.140 --> 00:51:31.650
I mean, you go, we've learned
so much through the process.

00:51:31.650 --> 00:51:35.730
We're just talkin' to my
mom multiple times a week

00:51:35.730 --> 00:51:38.610
and just seeing how slowly things move

00:51:38.610 --> 00:51:40.780
when you're trying to recover like that.

00:51:40.780 --> 00:51:43.100
But, for him to be back at
home was a big milestone.

00:51:43.100 --> 00:51:48.100
So, it was certainly one
of the biggest headlines

00:51:48.530 --> 00:51:51.002
for us all to be grateful
for seein' him there.

00:51:51.002 --> 00:51:54.613
For his side of the family,
he's got all his siblings

00:51:54.613 --> 00:51:56.174
that he wanted to visit.

00:51:56.174 --> 00:51:58.550
So, there was a question
whether he'd be able

00:51:58.550 --> 00:52:02.574
to leave the house and have
enough strength to go visit.

00:52:02.574 --> 00:52:03.407
Is he walking?

00:52:03.407 --> 00:52:08.407
He can walk some, but he just, his body

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:11.110
kinda wasted away as he was dealing

00:52:11.110 --> 00:52:13.430
with a series of infections that led

00:52:13.430 --> 00:52:16.180
to the near death experiences
that I was talkin' about.

00:52:17.420 --> 00:52:20.590
So yeah, it's just physically
gaining the strength

00:52:20.590 --> 00:52:23.040
to be able to walk, but he can stand.

00:52:23.040 --> 00:52:24.350
He can get in a wheelchair.

00:52:24.350 --> 00:52:27.260
They're doin' therapy at
home where he's continuing

00:52:27.260 --> 00:52:29.620
to regain strength, but he was able

00:52:29.620 --> 00:52:32.530
to go visit and be with his family

00:52:32.530 --> 00:52:36.540
when they got together on the next day.

00:52:36.540 --> 00:52:40.800
So, it was difficult going home.

00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:44.640
And then, we go to
Nanna's house and we had

00:52:44.640 --> 00:52:46.870
a big Thanksgiving spread there.

00:52:46.870 --> 00:52:48.801
It was absolutely amazing.

00:52:48.801 --> 00:52:51.710
And I remember talkin'
about our highlights

00:52:51.710 --> 00:52:53.640
of the year last year that Mom and Lewis

00:52:53.640 --> 00:52:54.473
came to that.

00:52:54.473 --> 00:52:59.473
They came to my Nanna's
house, my dad's mom's,

00:52:59.485 --> 00:53:01.930
I talked about that.

00:53:01.930 --> 00:53:03.501
But of course, they weren't
able to come to that.

00:53:03.501 --> 00:53:08.501
And there's a sense of,
when you only go back home,

00:53:09.900 --> 00:53:12.790
even at Nanna's where it
was a lot more positivity,

00:53:12.790 --> 00:53:16.910
it's still like okay,
there's still, you know,

00:53:16.910 --> 00:53:19.970
it's the second Thanksgiving
without my uncle

00:53:19.970 --> 00:53:23.410
and without papaw,
Nanna's lifelong partner.

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:30.170
So, there's a sense of loneliness and loss

00:53:30.170 --> 00:53:31.800
that still is very much there.

00:53:31.800 --> 00:53:34.790
And I think it was
important for us to be there

00:53:34.790 --> 00:53:37.690
and to talk about that, some part of it.

00:53:37.690 --> 00:53:40.110
So, it's like, you find yourself talkin'

00:53:40.110 --> 00:53:43.740
about those things as
well and my aunt saying

00:53:43.740 --> 00:53:45.830
she felt like that this
Thanksgiving was worse

00:53:45.830 --> 00:53:48.910
than the previous one
because when Thanksgiving

00:53:48.910 --> 00:53:50.940
rolled around last year
she was still in shock

00:53:50.940 --> 00:53:53.770
over the loss of her husband.

00:53:53.770 --> 00:53:57.850
So, in a lotta ways it was like oh, this

00:53:57.850 --> 00:54:00.636
is the new normal for us.

00:54:00.636 --> 00:54:04.453
But, you try to see it not
as, I'm tryin' to see it not

00:54:07.730 --> 00:54:11.570
as much as a downer, oh isn't this sad,

00:54:11.570 --> 00:54:15.760
but more like, it's sobering and it does

00:54:15.760 --> 00:54:18.110
provide an occasion for us to say

00:54:18.110 --> 00:54:22.113
what we can still be thankful for.

00:54:22.990 --> 00:54:24.360
But, it's not easy to do that.

00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:27.530
But I do think we tried
to, we tried to make

00:54:27.530 --> 00:54:28.380
the best of it.

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:31.180
We dug out those, remember
those old cornhole boards?

00:54:31.180 --> 00:54:35.310
When we did that commercial
for AJJ Cornhole,

00:54:35.310 --> 00:54:36.980
not the cornhole song, but we also did

00:54:36.980 --> 00:54:38.070
a commercial for them?

00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:39.820
We shot it at your grandmother's yard.

00:54:39.820 --> 00:54:40.653
In their yard.

00:54:40.653 --> 00:54:41.970
It's time you play Cornhole.

00:54:44.350 --> 00:54:46.000
Cornhole gives me confidence.

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:48.730
Well, I was going through
their shed out there

00:54:48.730 --> 00:54:52.050
and I found the Cornhole
set in the corn bags

00:54:52.050 --> 00:54:54.730
and they weren't all moldy and
we sat out there and played.

00:54:54.730 --> 00:54:57.249
It says AJJ Cornhole on the Cornhole.

00:54:57.249 --> 00:55:00.000
That was on ours, but apparently we got

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:03.800
a buncha free sets and we
left one at Nanna's house.

00:55:03.800 --> 00:55:05.320
So, we're out there playing Cornhole

00:55:05.320 --> 00:55:07.210
and havin' a good time.

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:12.210
But, there's, it's also
difficult when you just

00:55:12.590 --> 00:55:16.270
go back as a family once
a year, maybe twice.

00:55:16.270 --> 00:55:18.780
But, the last time we
had been back as a family

00:55:18.780 --> 00:55:21.750
was last Thanksgiving.

00:55:21.750 --> 00:55:24.380
So, you go back, it's been a whole year,

00:55:24.380 --> 00:55:27.520
everybody's a year older
and the older you are

00:55:27.520 --> 00:55:32.520
the more palpable that age
piling on is, you know.

00:55:34.440 --> 00:55:39.440
It's like sobering in that way.

00:55:41.420 --> 00:55:43.347
I got more gray every year.

00:55:43.347 --> 00:55:46.830
So then I just found
myself starting to think

00:55:46.830 --> 00:55:51.830
more about man, as my relatives get older,

00:55:53.690 --> 00:55:56.741
you start to think about,
what does care look like?

00:55:56.741 --> 00:55:59.850
What does care for my mom look like

00:55:59.850 --> 00:56:04.030
as she has no siblings
and I have no siblings

00:56:04.030 --> 00:56:06.890
and I live out here in Los Angeles?

00:56:06.890 --> 00:56:10.236
It's like, if it was
her going through that

00:56:10.236 --> 00:56:13.475
and not Lewis, as much
as I'd try to be there

00:56:13.475 --> 00:56:17.270
for her long distance over the phone

00:56:17.270 --> 00:56:19.700
and provide support, how
would that be different

00:56:19.700 --> 00:56:22.853
if it was mom that was havin' the strokes?

00:56:27.820 --> 00:56:29.310
I guess there's a lot to plan for.

00:56:29.310 --> 00:56:31.454
It's like, you can't over plan for things

00:56:31.454 --> 00:56:32.450
that you don't know exactly.

00:56:32.450 --> 00:56:34.730
You just gotta be ready for the unexpected

00:56:36.527 --> 00:56:37.540
and take it as it comes, right.

00:56:37.540 --> 00:56:38.720
But, I just couldn't help but start

00:56:38.720 --> 00:56:43.720
to think about, as relatives,
as loved ones get older,

00:56:45.350 --> 00:56:48.780
how do you start to interact with them?

00:56:48.780 --> 00:56:53.170
And there's a lotta technicalities,

00:56:53.170 --> 00:56:54.720
like what you're talkin' about.

00:56:56.080 --> 00:56:58.660
You know, with your Nanny, who's, well,

00:56:58.660 --> 00:56:59.840
she's in her 80s, right?

00:56:59.840 --> 00:57:00.673
Yeah.

00:57:00.673 --> 00:57:04.440
And she's getting her
will stuff together now,

00:57:04.440 --> 00:57:06.830
which is, this is what a
lot of people do, right,

00:57:06.830 --> 00:57:08.840
because there's not just the will.

00:57:08.840 --> 00:57:11.810
She had one, but then
you gotta update it, too.

00:57:11.810 --> 00:57:12.990
So, it's not that she didn't have one,

00:57:12.990 --> 00:57:14.590
but it needed to be drastically updated

00:57:14.590 --> 00:57:15.610
'cause it had been so long.

00:57:15.610 --> 00:57:19.123
And then, there is the end
of life care questions.

00:57:22.853 --> 00:57:23.686
Who's gonna take.

00:57:23.686 --> 00:57:27.283
At this point everybody's still kind of,

00:57:30.650 --> 00:57:35.100
no one's by themselves 'cause
your Nanny has her sister

00:57:35.100 --> 00:57:36.700
and then Lewis has your mom.

00:57:36.700 --> 00:57:37.533
Right.

00:57:37.533 --> 00:57:39.010
My parents have each other.

00:57:39.010 --> 00:57:42.433
So, it's like, what about when they don't?

00:57:43.370 --> 00:57:44.803
Yeah, and for that stuff, for all of that

00:57:44.803 --> 00:57:47.297
that happened to Lewis,
that was so unexpected

00:57:47.297 --> 00:57:49.913
at his age, you know.

00:57:49.913 --> 00:57:53.483
Especially when you have
grandparents that are living.

00:57:56.040 --> 00:57:59.010
Every year, for years
we go back and say well,

00:57:59.010 --> 00:58:00.640
you kinda think in the back of your mind,

00:58:00.640 --> 00:58:03.640
it's like, is this the
last, is this the last year

00:58:03.640 --> 00:58:06.440
that you see the oldest relatives?

00:58:06.440 --> 00:58:08.440
But then, there's the
things that last year

00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:09.930
that blind sided us.

00:58:09.930 --> 00:58:13.160
And then, this year, that blind
sided us with what happened

00:58:13.160 --> 00:58:18.160
to Lewis, so it's again, I
don't have any firm conclusions.

00:58:21.370 --> 00:58:25.010
I'm trying to find, there's still a lot

00:58:25.010 --> 00:58:27.590
to be thankful for and I think we are

00:58:27.590 --> 00:58:30.100
and I'm certainly glad that we went home,

00:58:30.100 --> 00:58:34.580
but it's, I don't know, it's a reminder

00:58:35.560 --> 00:58:38.050
that as much as you can't plan for it,

00:58:38.050 --> 00:58:40.070
there's some things you can like havin'

00:58:40.070 --> 00:58:42.490
your will in place and talking about it

00:58:43.460 --> 00:58:46.912
so that people know what your wishes are.

00:58:46.912 --> 00:58:51.240
I think it's good for all of us.

00:58:51.240 --> 00:58:54.040
I know that we had to pull
our wills together years ago.

00:58:55.220 --> 00:58:57.080
The younger you are the more fun it is

00:58:57.080 --> 00:58:58.840
to pull your will together
'cause it makes you

00:58:58.840 --> 00:59:00.890
feel good that you're
gettin' it outta the way.

00:59:00.890 --> 00:59:03.280
But the older you get, it starts to feel

00:59:03.280 --> 00:59:05.550
a little bit more bleak, you know.

00:59:05.550 --> 00:59:08.390
People just don't, I mean, a lotta people

00:59:08.390 --> 00:59:10.806
just put that stuff off.

00:59:10.806 --> 00:59:14.070
They don't wanna have those conversations.

00:59:14.070 --> 00:59:17.812
I mean, just the conversation
that you have to have,

00:59:17.812 --> 00:59:19.730
as you get to be middle
age, the conversation

00:59:19.730 --> 00:59:22.253
you have to have with your parents about,

00:59:26.236 --> 00:59:27.130
it would be a conversation I would have

00:59:27.130 --> 00:59:30.628
with my parents and also my brother.

00:59:30.628 --> 00:59:32.156
You figure out, okay, what are we gonna,

00:59:32.156 --> 00:59:35.540
when it gets to a place where our parents

00:59:35.540 --> 00:59:40.533
can't take care of themselves,
what does that look like?

00:59:40.533 --> 00:59:42.330
Yeah.

00:59:42.330 --> 00:59:45.360
One of them gonna be
livin' with you, with me?

00:59:45.360 --> 00:59:47.390
Are they gonna be, are we gonna find some

00:59:47.390 --> 00:59:48.445
kinda care for them?

00:59:48.445 --> 00:59:52.610
Those are really difficult
conversations to have,

00:59:52.610 --> 00:59:55.360
but you kinda, what you don't wanna do is

00:59:55.360 --> 00:59:57.840
you don't wanna be having
to have those conversations

00:59:57.840 --> 00:59:59.190
in the midst of the crisis.

00:59:59.190 --> 01:00:00.213
Right.

01:00:00.213 --> 01:00:03.575
I sound like an insurance salesman.

01:00:05.551 --> 01:00:07.151
But, I mean it's true, you know.

01:00:08.580 --> 01:00:11.060
You gotta have those
difficult conversations.

01:00:11.060 --> 01:00:12.510
Right.

01:00:12.510 --> 01:00:15.860
But I mean, we're stayin'
with Christy's sister

01:00:17.200 --> 01:00:18.270
and we had a great time there.

01:00:18.270 --> 01:00:22.020
And then, it's nice 'cause
my nephew, Nehemiah,

01:00:22.020 --> 01:00:26.983
he's like a little nut runnin' around.

01:00:28.170 --> 01:00:32.023
So, it's nice to have the kids in the mix.

01:00:33.610 --> 01:00:35.510
He and Lando were havin' a great time.

01:00:37.860 --> 01:00:39.160
But yeah, it was a bit of,

01:00:40.570 --> 01:00:43.870
I don't know, also not being there,

01:00:43.870 --> 01:00:46.470
so then, when you go back it's like,

01:00:46.470 --> 01:00:50.150
oh wow, it's like, everybody's older.

01:00:50.150 --> 01:00:52.830
Ain't nobody gettin' younger.

01:00:52.830 --> 01:00:56.443
So, it just feels, it feels like man,

01:00:57.650 --> 01:01:02.220
you're not an active part
of your loved ones lives

01:01:02.220 --> 01:01:04.320
or you're a much less active part of it

01:01:04.320 --> 01:01:07.563
just because of the shear
distance associated with it.

01:01:08.399 --> 01:01:10.780
And it's just the way it is.

01:01:10.780 --> 01:01:14.853
And I think at this point
it's the life that we live.

01:01:15.710 --> 01:01:17.260
It's not unusual.

01:01:17.260 --> 01:01:19.187
It's not wrong.

01:01:19.187 --> 01:01:21.250
But, you know what it is?

01:01:21.250 --> 01:01:25.600
It's very unusual in the
course of human history

01:01:26.520 --> 01:01:31.430
because it isn't until very recently,

01:01:31.430 --> 01:01:35.480
like a blink of an eye in the time scale

01:01:35.480 --> 01:01:37.570
of human history where people
like, what do you mean?

01:01:37.570 --> 01:01:41.620
You don't live at the same
place with your parents?

01:01:41.620 --> 01:01:42.600
Yeah.

01:01:42.600 --> 01:01:44.143
Everyone did that.

01:01:47.013 --> 01:01:49.370
And obviously, that's still
the case in a lotta cultures,

01:01:49.370 --> 01:01:51.530
but especially here.

01:01:51.530 --> 01:01:54.943
It's the exception to
the rule if you live.

01:01:59.214 --> 01:02:01.190
A lot of people are still
kind of close in proximity,

01:02:01.190 --> 01:02:03.750
but a lotta people were
actually in the same homes

01:02:04.770 --> 01:02:07.940
with multi-generational homes.

01:02:07.940 --> 01:02:08.773
Yeah, I don't know.

01:02:08.773 --> 01:02:10.320
We haven't really figured out what,

01:02:13.120 --> 01:02:17.400
like the expectation that,
I think the expectation

01:02:17.400 --> 01:02:21.960
still holds that your family
is gonna be responsible

01:02:21.960 --> 01:02:26.820
for your care in one sense,
but that doesn't mean

01:02:26.820 --> 01:02:29.270
what it meant for most of history,

01:02:29.270 --> 01:02:32.790
which is directly
responsible for your care.

01:02:32.790 --> 01:02:34.630
It's more like, no, no,
we're gonna get someone

01:02:34.630 --> 01:02:38.823
who's job it is to care for old people.

01:02:40.150 --> 01:02:41.720
I mean, there's a huge industry, right?

01:02:41.720 --> 01:02:42.690
Right.

01:02:42.690 --> 01:02:46.160
It's just, it's a weird
thing because it wasn't

01:02:46.160 --> 01:02:46.993
ever like that.

01:02:46.993 --> 01:02:48.790
It wasn't oh, there are
people whose job it is

01:02:48.790 --> 01:02:51.477
to come to our village and
take care of the old people.

01:02:51.477 --> 01:02:52.670
No, we do that.

01:02:52.670 --> 01:02:53.503
Yeah.

01:02:54.570 --> 01:02:56.887
And I haven't, again, I'm starting,

01:02:56.887 --> 01:03:00.240
I've been prompted
because of this experience

01:03:00.240 --> 01:03:03.220
to think about it, but
it's not like I've started

01:03:03.220 --> 01:03:04.070
to figure it out.

01:03:06.280 --> 01:03:09.163
But I do know that I'm not
like, well, I wouldn't,

01:03:10.790 --> 01:03:12.512
that sounds gross, or
I would never do that

01:03:12.512 --> 01:03:17.512
or passing preemptive judgment
on what other people do.

01:03:19.710 --> 01:03:21.360
I know it's gonna be complicated.

01:03:22.590 --> 01:03:25.180
But, thinking about it
a little bit at a time

01:03:25.180 --> 01:03:26.990
is better than just
continuing to put it off.

01:03:26.990 --> 01:03:29.853
So, I think that's one
of the applications.

01:03:31.900 --> 01:03:33.863
And I think another one is just being,

01:03:34.748 --> 01:03:36.240
I'm very grateful that we went back

01:03:36.240 --> 01:03:41.240
and that the time that we
spent there was quality.

01:03:41.732 --> 01:03:45.280
It was special at each place.

01:03:45.280 --> 01:03:50.280
I remember, I choose to
hang on to the moments

01:03:52.250 --> 01:03:56.160
and the memories like when I dozed off

01:03:56.160 --> 01:04:01.160
after eating all the
Bojangles on the couch bed

01:04:01.420 --> 01:04:04.380
that Nanny sleeps on in the living room.

01:04:04.380 --> 01:04:09.380
And I woke up to Lando and
my aunt just dying laughing

01:04:10.870 --> 01:04:14.210
because Lando was showing her how to use

01:04:15.508 --> 01:04:19.320
the face filter thingys on I don't know

01:04:19.320 --> 01:04:20.940
if it's Snapchat or Instagram.

01:04:20.940 --> 01:04:23.070
I don't even use that stuff.

01:04:23.070 --> 01:04:25.940
But, she was cackling.

01:04:25.940 --> 01:04:27.477
This was her first experience with it.

01:04:27.477 --> 01:04:28.460
First experience with it.

01:04:28.460 --> 01:04:31.213
So, it's like you eat
your food, you doze off

01:04:31.213 --> 01:04:33.629
and then you wake up
to that and it's like,

01:04:33.629 --> 01:04:36.870
that's what we're here for.

01:04:36.870 --> 01:04:38.040
Here for those moments when.

01:04:38.040 --> 01:04:39.140
Here for those filters.

01:04:39.140 --> 01:04:41.330
You're here for those
moments when everybody

01:04:41.330 --> 01:04:45.240
can share laughing, everybody can share,

01:04:45.240 --> 01:04:47.460
yeah, we know we're sharing the hurt

01:04:47.460 --> 01:04:49.760
of whatever it is that we're all feeling,

01:04:49.760 --> 01:04:53.160
but then at the same
time, we can also laugh

01:04:53.160 --> 01:04:57.583
at how stupid we look with
these face filters on.

01:04:58.630 --> 01:05:03.050
Did, was there turkey
served at Nanna's house?

01:05:03.899 --> 01:05:05.210
Yeah.

01:05:05.210 --> 01:05:06.990
So, some people did consume turkey.

01:05:06.990 --> 01:05:11.703
But, my dad brought a pork,

01:05:12.540 --> 01:05:15.690
he had smoked a pork,
a bunch of pork loins

01:05:16.830 --> 01:05:18.860
the week before for somethin' else

01:05:18.860 --> 01:05:20.490
and he had a few more.

01:05:20.490 --> 01:05:22.050
So, he heated one of those up and that's

01:05:22.050 --> 01:05:24.180
what I ate and it was amazing.

01:05:24.180 --> 01:05:25.013
Ham man.

01:05:28.250 --> 01:05:30.000
Got a lot to be thankful for.

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:32.120
Yeah, I'm thankful for
everything that we've had

01:05:32.120 --> 01:05:33.570
and the things that we don't have anymore,

01:05:33.570 --> 01:05:35.640
I'm thankful that I once had it

01:05:35.640 --> 01:05:38.980
and there was still a lot that we did have

01:05:38.980 --> 01:05:40.053
and to be had.

01:05:41.510 --> 01:05:44.500
And carry that spirit of thankfulness

01:05:44.500 --> 01:05:47.050
through the seasons.

01:05:47.050 --> 01:05:51.090
Don't just be thankful
one day outta the year.

01:05:51.090 --> 01:05:53.670
Be thankful every day.

01:05:53.670 --> 01:05:57.340
Be thankful that another day has happened

01:05:58.460 --> 01:05:59.470
